Date: Fri, 18 Jun 93 01:00:20 -0700
From: Inigo Montoya <inigo@u.washington.edu>
Subject: HODGEPODGE: cryonics, Jurassic Park, islands, SF encyclopedia

Tim May reposted his altinst post, and mentions an email message I
sent him.  

Oddly enough, when I got caught up on the list tonight, I noticed
that someone else, independently, mentioned a bill proposal in some
state which would make everyone a "Presumed Donor" unless you opted
out.   This neatly ties in with the issues I raised in connection
with a market for organs.  The state has significant incentive to
legislate in a way that makes more organs available (up to saturation,
presumably -- which never occurs in Niven's universe, since organ
replacement is The Means to Extend Life) -- even if that interferes
with our right to control our own physical bodies. 

One of the first reasonable things to see threatened would be
cryonically stored individuals; corpsicles could conceivably
be "mined" for organs.

Niven's society, however, has gone well beyond this.  Nearly every
crime -- down to jaywalking -- has capital punishment (or dismemberment
depending on your point of view).  A fascinating, if chilling, picture.
Yet another argument against statism.

The discussion about speciation reminds me of a book I'm reading, 
_The Flight of the Iguana_, by David Quammen.  The essay of the title
talks a good deal about island biology:  the types of species that
can travel to distant islands, and how their existence over time in
isolation (away from large predators) shapes them.  When humans
(with other predators in tow) show up, the odd locals tend to disappear
rapidly; Quammen notes that one estimate has 127 species of birds
extinct since 1680 -- 116 of which lived on islands.  There are
very good reasons why a cushy existence isn't good for one.

In connection with the Jurassic Park theme (ignoring, for the
moment, the implausibilities involved in ever getting enough
nucleic DNA from the minute amount of red blood cells that MIGHT
be found in a mosquito, that almost immediately got preserved
in amber -- not to mention having to duplicate -- with little
to help -- the proper environment for the embryo), at some point
in the future, we might *want* nasty, mean, vicious velociraptors
around (if we ever wind up becoming peacelovin', no more war-killing-
murdering of fellow humans types) to keep us from getting soft, 
exposing us to the dangers of extinction when our island is
broached.  On the other hand, given the proclivities of other
extropians, I doubt the problem will ever become that severe.
We'll probably come up with sufficiently hazardous toys of our own.
But it's worth considering that these horrible, destructive
things we invent might ultimately be good for the species.

And, still further off topic, the 2nd ed of the Encyclopedia of
Science Fiction is now out.  Don't bother unless you're desperate;
the error rate is really high (for entries I'm knowledgable about,
it's running between 1 in 4 and 1 in 5.  All kinds, too: incorrect
plot synopses, books placed in wrong series, incorrect biographical
data -- and very incomplete biographical data, too).  The entry
on libertarian sf is really pretty good, tho; lots of stuff cross-references
in from throughout the volume, too (which is good -- but they don't
all cross-reference out, which is annoying).  Review quality, imo,
is running between mediocre and good.

And I'd like to post a plug for the altinst list; the S/N ratio is
the best I've seen yet on any mailing list.  You might consider
subscribing, even if you have no intention of contributing; it's
fascinating reading.  Kudos to Robin Hanson.

