I've been quietly thinking about this from time to time
myself. I think it's the downside of that self-confidence
which is a critical part of extropian (and, more generally,
other self-governing) thought. If you've got far too much
experience dealing with fools--and who here does not?--then
it's just too darned easy to treat a source of strong
irritation here on the list as another fool.

Personally, I favor a presumption-of-innocence approach. I
know that the world is full of fools. But I also know that
some people can and do do better, given a bit of chance, so
I'll keep up polite restraint until I'm _really_ sure the
other person is a hopeless case. Then I amuse myself. But not
before.

It appears that Eric's jab at vegetarianism would have
collapsed, if countered with something like "This is
excessive, and strikes me as offensive. Did you _mean_ it this
way?" (Or rather, launched a more entertaining and possibly
more constructive thread.) Ditto for the other flamefests I've
watched.

And like you, I enjoy enough of what the major flamers post to
feel significant disappointment when they stoop to something
less than maturity. (Silliness can be mature; flame never is.)


