Eric S. Raymond says:
> > Why no fossil record if its true? I was under the impression there was
> > a pretty continuous fossil record of hominids on land in Africa from
> > several million years ago all the way up to Homo Sapiens Neanderthalis
> > -- and the fossils don't seem to indicate much in the way of otterlike
> > stages. Also, if we spent so much time as semi-aquatic beasts, why the
> > very recent divergence from Chimps (Pan Troglodites for those of you
> > who like proper scientific names), which is what the DNA clock folks
> > are currently claiming?
> 
> Depends on what you mean by "continuous".  I presume you're aware that 
> (in one famous simile) the entire corpus of physical evidence for human
> evolution could fit on your coffee table?

It wouldn't. There are so many fossils at this point that they'd
require a reasonable size room. Take the last group of five Homo
Sapiens Neanderthalis skeletons found in Israel a few months ago alone
-- I seriously doubt they would fit there, and thats just one recent
find. If we include beyond just the fossils things like artifacts (of
which there are huge numbers at this point -- I recall one apparent
Homo Erectus tool manufacture site as having thousands of implements
on its own) I think that you'd have a great deal of difficulty fitting
everything even into a large size house without grinding it down
to powder and evenly distributing it throughout.

> Anyhow, the aquatic-phase theory has never predicted much that would show
> in the fossil record; the major adaptations we had time to make (like the
> swimming nose, small webs between fingers, etc.) are all soft-tissue stuff.

I would seriously doubt it. Physiologically, swimming creatures tend
to have their connection between skull and spinal column towards the
back of the head so their eyes can face forward as they swim. All
humanoid skulls found back several million years seem to have their
heads set up for an upright or nearly upright existance.

Swimming mammals also have visible changes in their bones of their
appendages, which humanoid fossils have not revealed.

There are a number of other simple things that you don't see in
humanoid skeletons in this vein.

> It may be that the only accessible record of the aquatic phase is our
> own present-day physiology and instincts.  It is simple, observable fact that
> humans are much more water-loving and better swimmers than any other primate.

Thats patently false. Most primates can and do swim at times.
Certainly many other primates spend a lot more of their lives in the
water -- there are, for instance, the macaques that spend most of the
daylight hours in the wintertime in the hot springs of northern Japan.
I could give other examples.

> The theory doesn't propose any revisions of the hominid phylogenetic tree
> either.  Recent divergence from Bonzo only makes the question it tries to
> answer --- why are we so *different* from chimps --- more interesting.

Fully erect, better hands, larger brain, year-round breeding. Its not
actually that much of a stretch. Chimps can use simple tools, you
know, and demonstrate primitive language capability when taught ASL.
They are highly intelligent, too.

