Being able to hold our breaths isn't the big issue here.

Ever had your sinuses flooded with water?  It's quite unpleasant and
can be dangerous.  The human nose, unlike any other land-mammal nose (but
conspicuously like a seal's nose) is designed so it doesn't let in water
when immersed (unless you try to swim upside down and lose the trapped air
bubble; even then, second-order effects involving surface tension do a fair
job of holding onto it).

This is the reason (I'm inclined to believe) for the beaklike cartiligenous
structure we have on the front of our faces.  No other land mammal has anything
like this.
